course: dictionary
lesson: 3683
title: Flame: fire, danger and feeling
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A flame is the hot, bright gas you see in a fire, like the flame of a candle. «The fire crackled and sent up a spurt of flame, and he woke with a start.» The clues are crackled, fire and spurt, so flame means the bright, hot part of the fire you can see.
explain: Flame can also be a verb. To flame means to burn brightly or turn bright red. «The tiger's orange stripes seem to glow like flames in the dark forest» shows the stripes look bright and powerful, like fire in the dark.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «A family left a pan of oil on the hob and it caught fire, and a ball of flame burst up.» If you swap in "a ball of hot, bright gas", the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A flame is hot, bright burning gas that you can see.
fact: A flame needs fuel, oxygen and heat to burn; this is called the fire triangle.
fact: The blue part of a gas flame is the hottest part.
fact: «In flames» means burning, as in «go up in flames».
fact: Stay safe: keep hair, sleeves and paper away from flames, and never leave a candle burning alone. If clothes catch fire, «stop, drop and roll».
exercise: «The fire crackled and sent up a spurt of flame.» What is a flame? | *A hot, bright burning gas you can see | A sort of loud noise | A cold, grey puff of smoke | A dish of hot soup
exercise: «The old barn went up in flames.» What does «in flames» mean? | Painted bright red | Very cold and frozen | Made of solid gold | *Burning
exercise: «The tiger's orange stripes seem to glow like flames in the dark forest.» Which word tells you the stripes look bright, like burning fire? | forest | tiger | dark | *flames
test: «Keep long hair tied back near a flame.» Why should long hair be tied back? | So the flame can see you better | To make the hair grow thicker | So it looks neater in photos | *So it cannot catch fire
test: «The sunset made the sky flame red.» What does flame mean here? | To put out | To cool down | *To turn bright red | To fall softly
exam: What does a flame need to burn, besides heat? | *Fuel and oxygen | Sound and light | Water and sand | Sugar and salt
